# XSH sigsuspend()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.

assertion  XSH.sigsuspend.001
anchor     functions/sigsuspend.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       sigsuspend() with an empty mask, while SIGUSR1 is blocked and
           pending, runs the handler, then returns -1 with errno EINTR,
           and the mask is again the one before the call (SIGUSR1
           blocked).
test       t/001-handler-restores.c

assertion  XSH.sigsuspend.002
anchor     functions/sigsuspend.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       With nothing pending, sigsuspend() does not return until a caught
           signal sent 300 ms later is delivered (a 5 s SIGALRM watchdog
           turns a missed wakeup into a FAIL).
test       t/002-waits.c

assertion  XSH.sigsuspend.003
anchor     functions/sigsuspend.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       The mask before the call blocks SIGUSR1 and SIGUSR2. While
           sigsuspend() waits with a mask that blocks only SIGUSR2, a
           SIGUSR2 sent first stays pending and a later SIGUSR1 ends the
           wait; SIGUSR2 is handled only once the test unblocks it after
           the call. A 5 s SIGALRM watchdog turns a wait that SIGUSR1
           cannot end into a FAIL.
test       t/003-mask-during.c

assertion  XSH.sigsuspend.004
anchor     functions/sigsuspend.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       A process in sigsuspend() that gets a signal whose action is to
           terminate (SIGTERM at SIG_DFL, blocked outside the call) does
           not return: it ends by that signal.
test       t/004-terminates.c

assertion  XSH.sigsuspend.005
anchor     functions/sigsuspend.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       sigsuspend() with every signal but a pending, caught SIGUSR1 in its
           mask (SIGKILL and SIGSTOP included) waits without error and
           returns -1/EINTR after the handler. A child in sigsuspend() with
           every signal in the mask is still stopped by SIGSTOP (seen with
           WUNTRACED) and killed by SIGKILL.
test       t/005-unblockable.c

assertion  XSH.sigsuspend.006
anchor     functions/sigsuspend.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       With SIGUSR1 and SIGUSR2 blocked in two threads and a
           process-directed SIGUSR2 pending, the main thread's
           sigsuspend() with only SIGUSR2 in the mask (ended by a
           pthread_kill() of SIGUSR1) leaves SIGUSR2 pending on the
           process: the other thread still sees it in sigpending() and can
           accept it.
uses       XSH.sigpending.002
test       t/006-process-pending.c

assertion  XSH.sigsuspend.007
anchor     functions/sigsuspend.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       With SIGUSR1 ignored and SIGUSR2 caught and blocked, sigsuspend()
           with an empty mask stays suspended when a child sends SIGUSR1,
           and returns after the child then sends SIGUSR2 (300 ms later),
           its handler having run.
timeout    20
test       t/007-ignored.c
